---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  A spike's report: what one spike measured, how, on which branch its harness is kept, and
  any problem it found with the plan's design. The spike writes it as the document
  `<spike id>-report` of the plan's own project, so it travels to the board with the plan's
  other documents; the finalize planner reworks the plan from it, and every task that builds
  on the spike links it through the `plan-task` template's `spikes` answer.
variables:
  spike:
    description: >-
      The spike's node id, `spike-<topic>`.
    type: string
    required: true
  branch:
    description: >-
      The preserved branch the spike's work is kept on, `<host prefix>/<plan>/spike-<topic>`.
    type: string
    required: true
  harness:
    description: >-
      Where on that branch the harness is, and the one command that runs it to perform one
      measurement, so the implementing node can register it, or grow it into, the budget's
      onebudgetspec command.
    type: text
    required: true
  method:
    description: >-
      How the spike measured: the real systems it ran against, the workload it ran at, and
      anything that limits how far the numbers carry.
    type: text
    required: true
  candidates:
    description: >-
      One object per candidate budget the spike measured, with six string keys: `budget`
      (the candidate budget's id), `measure` (what was measured), `workload` (the workload
      it was measured at), `achievable` (what is achievable, with the measured figure),
      `limits` (what the system's limits allow, with the arithmetic, such as a rate limit
      divided across a run) and `consumed` (what the spike itself consumed: calls, quota,
      spend, time). `[]` for a spike that measured none itself, such as a harness spike
      other spikes start from.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  findings:
    description: >-
      One string per problem the spike found with the plan's design beyond its budgets: an
      approach that will not work, an API that does not behave as assumed, a missing
      dependency. `[]` when it found none.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: true
  visual_changes:
    description: >-
      One object per visual change the plan expects, for the reserved `spike-visual` spike,
      with four string keys: `name` (the change's short heading), `description` (what is
      changing, in plain words), `before` and `after` (each the bare file name of an image
      asset of this report, as its content references it with `./<name>`). `[]` for every
      other spike.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
---
{#- A table cell is one line holding no bare column delimiter, whatever an answer says. -#}
{% macro cell(value) %}{{ value | string | replace("|", "\\|") | replace("\r\n", " ") | replace("\n", " ") | replace("\r", " ") }}{% endmacro %}
{% block document_guidance %}
{#
  THE READERS. The finalize planner reads this report to rework the plan's targets,
  decomposition and contracts; the worker of every task that builds on the spike starts
  from it, and reaches the harness on the branch it names.

  A HARNESS SPIKE. A spike other spikes depend on and start from usually measures no
  candidate budget itself, so an empty `candidates` renders as a sentence saying so rather
  than a table with a header and no rows. Its dependencies are not recorded here: they are
  the spikes project's own edges, and a report copying them would be a second graph.

  A VISUAL SPIKE. The reserved `spike-visual` answers `visual_changes`, one entry per
  expected change, and gives each image named there to the store with `--asset`; it answers
  `candidates: []` unless it also measured something. Every other spike leaves the answer
  out, and its report renders no `## Visual changes` section.

  THIS TEMPLATE. Guidance is always a Jinja comment, never an HTML comment, so a rendered
  document carries none of it.
#}
{% endblock %}
# Spike `{{ spike }}`

- **Branch:** `{{ branch }}`

## Harness

{{ harness }}

## Method

{{ method }}

## Candidate budgets

{% if candidates %}
| Budget | Measure | Workload | Achievable | Limits allow | Consumed |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
{% for candidate in candidates %}
| {{ cell(candidate.budget) }} | {{ cell(candidate.measure) }} | {{ cell(candidate.workload) }} | {{ cell(candidate.achievable) }} | {{ cell(candidate.limits) }} | {{ cell(candidate.consumed) }} |
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
This spike measured no candidate budget itself.
{% endif %}

## Design findings

{% for finding in findings %}
- {{ finding }}
{% else %}
This spike found no problem with the plan's design beyond its budgets.
{% endfor %}
{% if visual_changes %}

## Visual changes
{% for change in visual_changes %}

### {{ change.name }}

{{ change.description }}

